[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-03", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 03 — one commit one pack, and the price of the catalog", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-03.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 03 - one commit, one pack, and the price of the catalog\n\n> Seeds from iteration 02: \"measure the crossover against a WRITE policy rather\n> than a shard count\" and \"the catalog lookup is unpriced\".\n\nIteration 02 produced the many-pack regime by SPLITTING one snapshot into P\npacks. That is not what ADR-2608160100 proposes. A commit is not a shard: it\nwrites only novelty, and a prolly tree shares every subtree it did not change,\nso the blocks a query touches at commit N were written across commits 1..N in\nwhatever proportion the sharing produced.\n\nThis builds real successive commits (`arr/commit!` with `prev`), packs each\ncommit's newly-written blocks, and asks the same query of the head. The three\nrare subjects are in the FIRST commit, so the answer stays 3 rows while history\ngrows AND the query is forced to reach back to the oldest commit.\n\n## Measured\n\n  commits  per  blocks  per-object | packs opened reads catalog total   N/P    vs\n        1  800      23          12 |     1      1     8       1     9  8.00  1.33x\n        2  400      32          12 |     2      2    10       2    12  5.00  1.00x\n        4  200      49          12 |     4      2    10       2    12  5.00  1.00x\n        8  100      82          12 |     8      3    12       3    15  4.00  0.80x\n       16   50     144          12 |    16      3    12       3    15  4.00  0.80x\n       32   25     270          12 |    32      3    12       3    15  4.00  0.80x\n\n## H2 is refuted, and that is the good news\n\nThe hypothesis was that one-commit-one-pack puts a query over a database with a\nhistory into the losing regime, \"because it touches as many packs as commits\".\n\nIt does not. **Packs opened saturates at 3 and stays there from 8 commits to\n32**, while the database grows from 82 blocks to 270. A query descends a tree,\nand the nodes on that path were written by a handful of commits -- the most\nrecent one that rewrote the spine, the oldest one that still owns the shared\nleaves, and about one in between. The count is bounded by the tree, not by the\nhistory.\n\nSo the fear that a long history multiplies pack opens is wrong, and the\n`shard`-based sweep in iteration 02 was measuring a distribution that a real\nwrite policy does not produce.\n\n## The catalog is what decides it, and iteration 02's rule is wrong without it\n\nBoth earlier benches used an in-memory map for CID -> pack. ADR-2608160100 puts\nthat catalog on the datom plane, so the first lookup of each pack is a request.\nPriced here, it is `+1 per distinct pack`, and it changes the arithmetic:\n\n    packed  =  2P (open) + N (blocks) + P (catalog)  =  3P + N\n    per-object = 2N\n\nso packing wins while **N/P > 3**, not `N/P > 2`. Iteration 02's rule was\nderived from a bench that did not pay for the catalog, and it is off by exactly\nthat term. Every row above matches `3P + N`.\n\nAt the measured shape of a real history -- N = 6 blocks read, P = 3 packs\nopened, N/P = 4.00 -- packing is **0.80x**, a loss. Without the catalog it is\n12 vs 12, parity. The catalog is not a rounding error on this workload; it is\nthe whole result.\n\n## What this changes\n\nIteration 02 concluded \"pack a group only when a query reads more than two of\nits blocks\". That number is now three, and the reason for the difference is a\ncost that the earlier bench had explicitly declared unmeasured. The loop\ncorrected its own published rule by pricing the thing it had said was unpriced.\n\nIt does not overturn the shape of iteration 02's conclusion -- the same\nsubstrate still answers hydration and index-lookup oppositely -- but it moves\nthe line, and it moves it against packing.\n\n## What this does NOT say\n\n  - Still nothing in production writes a pack.  [WITHDRAWN 2026-09-09, later the same day] The correction that stood here was itself wrong, and the original sentence above is RIGHT. It said `net-kotobase/engine` carries the pack namespaces and sets KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES=1 in its testnet env, and concluded pack writes were LIVE there. The first half is true and the conclusion does not follow: a source tree is not a deployment. Measured from outside with no credential -- backend.kotobase.net/_health answers 503 for three missing secrets (KOTOBASE_B2_KEY_ID, KOTOBASE_B2_APP_KEY, KOTOBA_SEED), and kotobase-cf-wasm-testnet.aozora.app/_health answers 200 with fields (canonical_cloudflare_independent, object_store) that exist only in net-kotobase/control-plane/kotobase-graph-database's worker, a tree with NO pack namespaces at all. Both trees declare an env.testnet with the same worker name, so a deploy from either replaces the other, and control-plane deployed last. So nothing in production or testnet writes or reads a pack, and these iterations are evidence for a plane that is still unwired. Root ADR-2608170300 carries the measurement.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 04\n\n  - Measure the hydration chain directly: N large, P small is the regime the\n    rule now says to pack, and it is the one nobody has benched end to end.\n  - Is the catalog cacheable across queries? If a Worker holds it, the term\n    drops out and the crossover returns to 2. That is a deployment question\n    with a measurable answer.\n  - Pack opens saturated at 3 for every history length tested. Find where it\n    stops saturating -- deeper trees, wider queries -- or find that it does not.\n"}]
